## 3. Off-stationary cutpoints are decidable

Consider the strict cutpoint problem

\[
\exists w:
\mu P_wf>\lambda.
\]

Define the stationary baseline

\[
b=\nu f.
\]

Suppose

\[
\lambda\ne b.
\]

Let

\[
\gamma=|\lambda-b|>0.
\]

Choose \(N\) such that

\[
\operatorname{osc}(f)\rho^N TV(\mu,\nu)<\gamma.
\]

For example,

\[
\boxed{
N
=
\left\lceil
\frac{
\log\left(
\gamma/(
\operatorname{osc}(f)TV(\mu,\nu)
)
\right)
}{
\log\rho
}
\right\rceil
}
\]

when the ratio is below \(1\).

Then:

### Case \(\lambda>b\)

Every word with \(|w|\ge N\) satisfies

\[
\mu P_wf<\lambda.
\]

So one only needs to inspect finitely many words of length \(<N\).

### Case \(\lambda<b\)

Every sufficiently long word satisfies

\[
\mu P_wf>\lambda.
\]

Hence the answer is automatically yes once arbitrarily long words are allowed.

Therefore:

### Theorem CW-002H.2 — Margin-to-stationarity decidability

For a finite uniformly contracting family with a common invariant law,

\[
\boxed{
\lambda\ne\nu f
\Longrightarrow
\text{strict cutpoint emptiness is decidable by a finite horizon}.
}
\]

The same principle applies to weak inequality with the expected boundary care.

---

## 4. The exact stationary cutpoint can remain undecidable

Now specialize to positive doubly-stochastic \(n\times n\) matrices.

The common invariant distribution is uniform:

\[
u=\frac1n\mathbf1.
\]

For a single accepting state \(j\),

\[
f=e_j,
\qquad
uf=\frac1n.
\]

Rote's theorem gives positive doubly-stochastic \(7\times7\) PFA instances for which deciding

\[
\exists w:
(\mu P_w)_j>\frac17
\]

is undecidable.

But every such finite positive generator family has

\[
\rho<1.
\]

Therefore:

### Theorem CW-002H.3 — Stationary-cutpoint singularity

There exist finite rational stochastic matrix families with a common stationary distribution and uniform exponential contraction such that:

- every strict cutpoint problem
  \[
  \lambda\ne\nu f
  \]
  is decidable by a finite contraction horizon;
- the exact stationary cutpoint
  \[
  \boxed{\lambda=\nu f}
  \]
  is undecidable.

This is a genuine algorithmic phase transition at zero margin.

---

## 5. Why contraction does not help at zero margin

Rote's construction makes the mechanism explicit.

He starts from matrices whose relevant scalar sign encodes a Post Correspondence Problem instance, then forms positive doubly-stochastic matrices

\[
D_i
=
(1-\alpha)J+\alpha C_i,
\]

where \(J\) is the completely mixing matrix.

For the relevant zero-sum deviation, products satisfy a relation of the form

\[
\text{deviation after }m\text{ letters}
=
\alpha^m
\times
\text{encoded algebraic signal}.
\]

After shifting the start vector into the probability simplex, the acceptance probability becomes

\[
\boxed{
\frac17
+
\alpha^{|w|}
s(w),
}
\]

where the sign of \(s(w)\) carries the undecidable PCP predicate.

Thus the signal amplitude tends exponentially to zero, but its sign remains computationally meaningful.

Dobrushin contraction controls the amplitude:

\[
|\alpha^{|w|}s(w)|\to0.
\]

It says nothing about deciding whether

\[
s(w)>0
\]

for some finite word.

So:

\[
\boxed{
\text{asymptotic stability}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{decidability of exact transient sign questions}.
}
\]

