## 6. Vanishing-signal computation

This motivates an independent concept.

A **vanishing-signal computation** is a switched/iterated system with:

1. a globally attracting reference state \(x_\ast\);
2. a uniform contraction law
   \[
   \|x_w-x_\ast\|\le C\rho^{|w|};
   \]
3. an exact predicate encoded only in the sign or side of a hyperplane:
   \[
   \ell(x_w-x_\ast)>0.
   \]

Rote's positive doubly-stochastic PFA construction is an explicit undecidable example.

The computational content survives only in an exponentially shrinking direction.

This phenomenon is potentially relevant far beyond automata:

- consensus systems;
- analog computation;
- switched linear systems;
- robust verification;
- numerical precision barriers.

---

## 7. A robustness corollary

Suppose the threshold has declared tolerance/margin \(\gamma>0\).

Then the undecidable stationary-cutpoint behavior disappears beyond a computable horizon.

Therefore any verification contract that asks only whether

\[
\mu P_wf\ge b+\gamma
\]

for fixed \(\gamma>0\) has finite search depth under uniform contraction.

The algorithmic pathology resides exactly in arbitrarily small deviations around the limiting boundary.

This gives a structural principle:

\[
\boxed{
\text{uniform contraction + nonzero decision margin}
\Rightarrow
\text{finite-horizon verification}.
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{zero margin}
\text{ can retain undecidable sign complexity}.
}
\]

