## 8. Positive doubly-stochastic computational core with one terminal readout

Rote's Theorem 1(a) can also be converted directly into a pair-synchronization threshold problem.

Take the \(7\)-state positive doubly-stochastic PFA with initial distribution \(\pi\), matrices \(D_i\), and single accepting state \(j\).

Add two absorbing states \(z,r\) and one deterministic terminal readout \(F\):

- accepting state \(j\to z\);
- every other PFA state \(\to r\);
- \(z,r\) fixed.

Extend every \(D_i\) by an identity block on \(z,r\).

Let

\[
p=(\pi,0,0),
\qquad
q=e_z.
\]

Then for every PFA word \(w\),

\[
\boxed{
TV(pD_wF,q)
=
1-\pi D_we_j.
}
\]

Words without \(F\) have distance \(1\), and after the first \(F\) the distance freezes.

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
\exists v:
TV(pP_v,qP_v)<\frac67
}
\]

is undecidable.

So a single deterministic readout interface is enough to recover undecidability even when all computational dynamics generators are positive doubly-stochastic.

This isolates the source of hardness:

\[
\text{contracting dynamics}
+
\text{exact terminal threshold/readout}.
\]

---

# Verdict

The correct boundary is not "positive matrices are easy."

Positive common-stationary dynamics make **asymptotic coalescence** easy.

But exact transient half-space/cutpoint questions can remain undecidable at the stationary baseline.

The sharp principle is:

\[
\boxed{
\lambda\ne\nu f
\Rightarrow
\text{finite-horizon decidability},
}
\]

while Rote gives:

\[
\boxed{
\lambda=\nu f
\Rightarrow
\text{undecidability can persist despite exponential mixing}.
}
\]

This is one of the strongest standalone byproducts of the CWR program so far.
